1. **Routes (D-1).** `/security` (a chooser: "Choose a customer to preview", one row per customer from `loadDirectory()` (`customer/useDirectory.ts` line 23, import), each a link to `/security/<routeId>/dashboard`, the label by the alias rule of item 6, never the name for a caller holding Demo) and `/security/[customerId]/{dashboard,fleet,assessments}`. Each page resolves the id with `customerForRoute` (imported; an unknown id calls `notFound()`, nothing echoed). No `layout.tsx`, no `loading.tsx` under `security/`. Every page renders through one shared `components/security/SecurityFrame.tsx`: a `PageHeader` (imported from `ui/PageHeader`, not edited) whose eyebrow is "Customer security preview" and whose customer line uses the item-6 label, then the banner (item 7), then the view.
2. **Customer menu (D-5), `customer-nav.ts` and a new `components/security/CustomerNav.tsx`.** The model's `built` type becomes `boolean`; `dashboard`, `fleet`, `assessments` become `built: true`; `findings`, `roadmap`, `reports`, `assurance` stay `built: false` (part B, HPSMPOC-388); **`frameworks` stays `built: false` with a new field `held: true` and a comment saying the view is held until a supplied and approved mapping exists** (no ticket key in product code: the comment says it in words). Hrefs become the route PATTERN `/security/[customerId]/<slug>` (so the existing `existsSync(APP, href, "page.tsx")` guards keep working literally), plus an exported `customerNavHref(item, routeId)` that fills the id. `CustomerNav.tsx` renders `nav aria-label="Customer security"` with the two groups (`role="group"` + `aria-labelledby`, as `GlobalNav.tsx` lines 115-119 do) and ONLY built entries, `aria-current="page"` on the current one, the same link classes as `GlobalNav.tsx` line 107 including `sidebar-ring` and `min-h-11`. With no customer in the path (`/security` itself) it renders the group labels and a single "Choose a customer" link to `/security`, so no entry points at a pattern.
3. **The switch and the mount (D-2), new `components/security/WorkspaceSwitch.tsx`, mounted from `Sidebar.tsx` line 72 only.** Line 72 becomes: the mode line ("Partner workspace" or "Customer security", a `p` in the same classes as `GlobalNav.tsx` line 117's group label), then `{customerMode ? <CustomerNav .../> : <GlobalNav .../>}`, then `<WorkspaceSwitch onNavigate={onNavigate} />`, where `customerMode` is `usePathname()` starting with `/security` (the route IS the state: no storage, no session field, no new context). The switch is a `div role="group" aria-label="Workspace view"` holding two `button type="button"` elements with `aria-pressed`, visible text "Partner view" and "Customer view", each `min-h-11 sidebar-ring` on the navy sidebar tokens; pressing one calls `router.push` (`next/navigation`) and `onNavigate`: Customer view from `/customers/<id>/...` goes to `/security/<id>/dashboard`, from anywhere else to `/security`; Partner view from `/security/<id>/...` goes to `/customers/<id>`, from `/security` to `/home`. Pressing the already-pressed one does nothing. **Offered only when `useSession().state.user?.role` is one of the four existing roles** (`ROLE_LABEL` keys, imported); for a null role or any other value it renders nothing (AC 4). Never a select of roles, never a "Customer" role.
4. **Reviewed findings (D-3), new `components/security/reviewed.ts`, the single source for part A and part B.** `export const REVIEWED_STATES = ["accepted", "amended", "risk-accepted"] as const` and `reviewedFindings(findings, dispositions)` returning, in `inSeverityOrder` (imported), only findings whose disposition state is in that list; `open` (untouched rule output, including every finding fired by an unreviewed "No" answer) and `suppressed` (the consultant's own "not a finding") are never returned. The amended severity shows through `effectiveSeverity` (imported). An amended narrative is NOT shown in this preview (it is a consultant's working text; the title and the rule's remediation action are). For an API customer the dispositions are the findings' own `disposition` fields; for the held customer they are `session.assessment.dispositions`. **No view derives a finding, a gap or a count of gaps from responses.**
5. **The three views.** (a) **Security dashboard** (`SecurityDashboard.tsx`): four tiles: "Reviewed findings" (the count from item 4, with a critical/high/medium/low split of reviewed ones only), "Questions answered" ("<n> of <m>" from `completion.answered/required` or, for the held customer, applicable questions minus `openQuestionCount(a)` from `lib/status.ts` line 16, imported; no hard-coded total), "Assessment status" (the stage words from `stageFromApi`/`stageFromPrototype`), "Declared devices and sites" (item 5b's numbers); then a "Priority reviewed findings" panel: the first four of item 4, each a severity `Tag` (imported), the finding title and the rule's remediation action with its bucket in words ("Act now", "Within 30 days", "Within 60 to 90 days", "Ongoing"); with none, the sentence "No consultant-reviewed findings yet. The assessment may still be in progress or awaiting review." No "All findings" link in part A (the findings view is part B; no dead end). **No score, no band, no domain panel and no Yes/No count** (D-4). (b) **Fleet and devices** (`FleetDevices.tsx`): tiles "Declared devices" (`printerCount`, else "Not recorded"), "Declared sites" (`siteCount`, else "Not recorded"), "Firmware inventory" "Not connected", "Policy coverage" "Not measured", each with a one-line source note ("From the customer's profile; not synchronised", "No printer management connection in this build"); then the design's "Estate discovery and evidence" table, five rows by three columns (Data point, Current source, Evidence an assessment needs), static words, as a semantic `table` with `th scope`. No sample values from `customer/engagement.ts` are shown here. (c) **Security assessments** (`SecurityAssessments.tsx`): one row or card per assessment of the customer, newest first (`createdAt`): the status words, "Started <date>", "Submitted <date>" when present, "<n> of <m> questions answered", and a review line: "Reviewed by a consultant" when the status is `reviewed` or `report-issued`, otherwise "Awaiting consultant review", plus the reviewed-finding count of that assessment. **No per-question answer table in part A** (D-4).
6. **Words for the customer (AC 5).** One hook in `components/security/` that turns `useCustomerShownAs(routeId)` (imported) into the label by the four-way rule of `CustomerShell.tsx` lines 59-68 (name for `name`, `aliasLabel(alias)` for `alias`, `NO_RUN_ALIAS_LABEL` for `no-run`, the neutral word "Customer" for `pending`), and a list variant for the chooser that asks `getMe` once and `getMetricsSummary` once per render with the same decision rule as `useCustomerShownAs.ts` lines 33-38 (a unit row proves both give the same label on the same mocks). The real name never reaches the DOM for a caller holding Demo: not in a heading, a link, a title attribute, an `aria-label`, a table cell or a hidden element, on any of the four pages; and the API result's `customer.name` is never rendered for anyone.
7. **Banner and words.** The only new customer-visible strings: the switch's "Workspace view", "Partner view", "Customer view"; the mode lines "Partner workspace" and "Customer security"; "Customer security preview"; the banner "This is a read-only preview of what a customer would see. Answers given in an assessment are self-reported and are not device verification. Only findings a consultant has reviewed appear here."; the chooser's "Choose a customer to preview" and "Choose a customer"; the tile, panel, table and review words of item 5. No price, no currency sign, no "cost", no "revenue", no tier price, no service offer or "next service" anywhere in `components/security/**` or the four pages (AC 2). No person's name, no decision-card id, no `B###` id, no `C-` entry number and no ticket key in any added line (comments and assertion messages included). All added text is ASCII (three full stops, never the ellipsis character; "and" never an ampersand in new words; the stub's existing labels "Fleet & devices" and "Reports & evidence" are kept as the design's labels).

## DRAFTER DECISIONS (D-n; accepted or changed by Tuesday at the stamp)
- **D-1** Routes `/security` (chooser) and `/security/[customerId]/<view>`, rather than one page with a customer select: the app's customer is always in the path (`/customers/[customerId]`), the route allow-list (`customerForRoute`) already exists, and part B adds sibling folders. No layout file (loading-states pin).
- **D-2** The switch and the mode line live in the SIDEBAR, after the menu, as two `aria-pressed` buttons. Reason: `AppShell.tsx` is frozen (the design's top-bar place is the one place you may not touch); two LINKS would break `AppShell.sidebar.test.tsx` line 219 (8 links in the aside), and a switch before the menu would break `shell-sidebar.spec.ts` line 227 (first Tab on Home); both files are not yours. The route is the state, so a reload, a deep link and the back button all agree. **If you find the switch cannot work from the sidebar** (for example it must be reachable while the sidebar is collapsed, and you judge that required), **STOP: `STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY the switch needs AppShell.tsx`**; never edit AppShell.
- **D-3** "Consultant-reviewed" = the finding's own disposition is accepted, amended or risk-accepted; open and suppressed are never shown. Reason: `open` is exactly the rule's untouched output (the "unreviewed No" of AC 1), `suppressed` is the consultant's decision that it is not a finding, and the design's own rule is `findings(c).filter(f=>f.approved)` (line 261). Alternative (also require the assessment status `reviewed` or `report-issued`): Q-2.
- **D-4** No number derived from unreviewed answers on any part-A view: no answer-based score, no domain panel, no Yes/No/Unsure counts, no per-question answer table. Reason: AC 1 is a disclosure rule and these are exactly the surfaces that would show unreviewed "No" answers as gap-shaped data; the app's questions are option-based, not Yes/No (the design's counts do not map), and the score is not needed for any AC. Alternative (build the design's per-question table with the words "Pending consultant review" and never "gap" or "finding" for an unreviewed No): Q-3.
- **D-5** The menu shows only built entries (three in part A); part B's four and the held Framework alignment are in the model and rendered for no one, as `GlobalNav`'s `built` rule does. Framework alignment is never built in this lane or by a flag flip: a unit row pins it (`built === false`, no page file, `held === true`).
- **D-6 Sibling landings.** You render `ui/PageHeader` and `ui/Tag` (frozen, not H3's) and you do not render `ui/Table` or `customer/parts.tsx`; if you choose to render `ui/Table` or `StagePill`, H3 (kit B929, seat H60) is changing them: expect a rebase-free sync onto its landing (below) before the gate. Rule (as B928): if a sibling has landed before your Step 3, base on that main. If one lands after your Step 3, do NOT rebase: Tuesday sends an ADDENDUM naming the new main and you bring it in by ONE `git merge --no-ff --no-edit <new main sha>` in `wt-b937-fix` (a new commit; the push is a fast-forward of your own ref; no rebase, no force), re-run Step 7 and the screenshots. A merge conflict is a STOP, except inside the two D-7 tail lines, where you keep both sides' segments (sorted) and your own threshold, and print the resolved lines in STATUS.
- **D-7 The serialized tail (H61, kit B930, seat H61, owns `nav-dead-ends.test.ts`; nobody owns `returnPath.ts`).** Your branch touches `nav-dead-ends.test.ts` line 26 and `returnPath.ts` `RETURN_SEGMENTS` ONLY on a base or merge that holds H61's landing (read its merged PR by `gh api -X GET 'repos/datasecau/HPSM-POC/pulls?state=closed&per_page=20'` and the `compare` file list). Then: line 26 becomes the true floor after both lanes, `toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(5)` with the comment "the five customer routes not built yet" (four of part B plus the held Framework alignment), and `RETURN_SEGMENTS` gains `"security"` in sorted place, nothing else in either file. **Until then** those two rows (`nav-dead-ends.test.ts` "every entry that is not built ...", `returnPath.test.ts` "the allowed first segments ...") are the ONLY expected reds: list them in STATUS as `D-7 pending` with their messages, finish every other step, push, and end `STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY D-7: has B930 landed (two tail lines wait for it)?`. Tuesday answers with an ADDENDUM naming the main to merge; you merge it (D-6 rule), make the two edits in one commit, re-run Step 7, and end READY. If H61 has already landed before your Step 3, base on it and make the two edits in your own commit C1.
